{
  "pdf_page": 350,
  "book_page": 320,
  "verified_by": "claude-opus-5",
  "verified_on": "2026-09-07",
  "entries": [
    {
      "entry": 0,
      "root": "Nârikelah, Nârikerah",
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "modern_form": "Sanskrit नारिकेल nārikela- / nārikera- (also nālikera-) 'coconut, coconut palm', a word of non-Indo-European (probably Dravidian/Austroasiatic) origin in Sanskrit",
      "note": "Root, language label and gloss are all right, and Nourai's own reference is the modern standard for this word: Klein p. 1028 s.v. narghile gives 'OI. nārikerah, nārikelah', adding that the word 'is of uncertain, possibly Dravidian, origin'. AHD's narghile entry has 'Sanskrit nārikelaḥ, nālikeraḥ'; Mo'in's footnote in Borhan-e Qate' p. 2095 gives 'در سانسکریت nârikela، nârikera و نظایر آن' with a reference to Laufer, Sino-Iranica p. 585. The one thing worth stressing is that the Sanskrit word is itself a loan into Indo-Aryan from a South Indian source, so the chart's ROOT is the earliest recoverable form, not an inherited IE root — Nourai does not claim otherwise. Both variants (-kelah / -kerah) are genuine Sanskrit doublets with the usual Indic l/r interchange.",
      "note_fa": "ریشهٔ این خانواده سنسکریت nārikela- / nārikera- «نارگیل، درخت نارگیل» است و برچسب زبان و معنای آن در نمودار درست است. کلاین (ص ۱۰۲۸) و فرهنگ AHD همین دو صورت را می‌آورند و هر دو تصریح می‌کنند که خودِ واژه در سنسکریت وام‌واژه است و احتمالاً از زبان‌های دراویدی جنوب هند به سنسکریت رفته است؛ پس این صورت، کهن‌ترین صورت بازیافتنی است نه ریشهٔ هندواروپایی. معین نیز در حاشیهٔ برهان قاطع (ص ۲۰۹۵) به سانسکریت nârikela و nârikera و به Sino-Iranica لوفر ص ۵۸۵ ارجاع می‌دهد. دوگانگی l/r در پایانهٔ واژه ابدال معمول هندی است. ادعای نورایی درست است.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.ahdictionary.com/word/search.html?q=narghile",
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B1%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%84"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {"ref": "KLN:1028", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966.txt line 24983, printed-page marker '1028' standing at line 24960 immediately above it, so the entry really is on p. 1028: 'narghile, nargile, n., hookah, an Oriental pipe. — Pers. nargil, \"coconut tree\"; prob. fr. OI. nārikerah, nārikelah, of s.m., which is of uncertain, possibly Dravidian, origin; so called because orig. narghiles were made of coconut shells.' Exactly the chart's root, both variants."}
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {"src": "AHD", "where": "online entry 'narghile' (= 1975 New College ed. p. 873)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'French narghilé, obsolete variant of narguilé, from Persian nārgīleh, from nārgīl, coconut (from which the receptacle was made), from Sanskrit nārikelaḥ, nālikeraḥ.'"},
        {"src": "BQT", "where": "p. 2095 s.v. نارگیل (Mo'in's footnote; vol. 4, pdf p. 178)", "stance": "supports", "note": "read from the page image (ocr/BQT/2095.png, saved to 2095.vision.txt): '… در سانسکریت nârikela، nârikera و نظایر آن. Laufe., Sino-Iranica, p.585'."},
        {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 1556", "stance": "partial", "note": "grep 'nargil' in LKT.txt line 3581: 'Pers. nārgilä: \"Wasserpfeife\" [angeblich aus sskr. nārikera \"Kokosnuß\"]' — Lokotsch marks the Sanskrit source 'angeblich' (allegedly), i.e. he reports it without endorsing it."},
        {"src": "HJB", "where": "s.v. COCO, COCOA, COCOA-NUT, pp. 228-229", "stance": "supports", "note": "HJB.txt lines 6834, 6839 quote Friar Jordanus c.1328 'First of these is a certain tree called Nargil …' and John Marignolli c.1350 'Now the Nargil is the Indian Nut' — the Indian word in medieval European travel writing."}
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Pahlavi",
          "words": "anârgîl",
